            {
              "fact": "F1",
              "source": "lem-qft-free-action-equations",
              "source_section": "Statement",
              "quote": "Fix $\\mu\\ge0$, $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, $\\Box=\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\partial_b$, and the Dirac matrices and adjoint of [[def-qft-dirac-projections]]; $\\mu$ is the inverse-length mathematical shell parameter of [[def-qft-scalar-mass-shell]]. For a smooth real scalar field $\\phi$ and smooth commuting complex spinor $\\psi:\\mathbb R^4\\to\\mathbb C^4$, use the normalized free Lagrangian densities\n$$\\mathcal L_\\phi=-\\frac12\\left(\\eta^{ab}\\partial_a\\phi\\,\\partial_b\\phi+\\mu^2\\phi^2\\right),\\qquad \\mathcal L_D=\\bar\\psi\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi,\\qquad \\bar\\psi=\\psi^\\dagger\\beta.$$\nFor compactly supported smooth real variations $h$ and compactly supported smooth complex spinor variations $\\chi$, the action symbols are used only through the following finite, compactly supported first variations; no global integrability of the background fields is assumed:\n$$\\delta S_\\phi(\\phi;h)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}h(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi\\,d^4x,\\qquad \\delta S_D(\\psi;\\chi)=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_{\\mathbb R^4}\\chi^\\dagger\\beta\\left(i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu\\right)\\psi\\,d^4x.$$\nEach field is stationary under all such variations if and only if its corresponding Euler\u2013Lagrange equation holds pointwise:\n$$(\\Box-\\mu^2)\\phi=0,\\qquad (i\\gamma^a\\partial_a-\\mu)\\psi=0.$$\nThe adjoint Dirac equation follows from the second equation because the fixed matrices satisfy $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$. These are mathematical variation identities for commuting classical fields; they assert no quantization, Grassmann calculus, or physical measurement.",
              "uses": [
                "1.2"
              ]
