              "claim": "From [F2], $\\beta=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,-I_2)$ is Hermitian and squares to $I_4$; each Pauli matrix is Hermitian, so $\\alpha_j^\\dagger=\\alpha_j$, and the block formulas give $\\alpha_j\\beta=-\\beta\\alpha_j$. Therefore $(\\gamma^0)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta^2=I_4=\\beta\\gamma^0$, while for $j=1,2,3$,\n$$ (\\gamma^j)^\\dagger\\beta=(\\beta\\alpha_j)^\\dagger\\beta=\\alpha_j\\beta^\\dagger\\beta=\\alpha_j=\\beta^2\\alpha_j=\\beta\\gamma^j. $$\nThis proves [F3] from the exact matrix definitions. Now vary $\\psi$ along a compactly supported complex $\\chi$ and $\\bar\\psi$ along $\\bar\\chi$. Expanding the four kinetic terms and integrating the terms containing $D_a\\chi$ or $D_a\\bar\\chi$ by parts gives\n$$\\delta S_D=2\\operatorname{Re}\\int_U\\chi^\\dagger\\beta R_D\\,d^4x, \\qquad R_D=(i\\gamma^aD_a-\\mu)\\psi.$$\nFor example, $\\int\\bar\\psi\\gamma^aD_a\\chi=-\\int(D_a\\bar\\psi)\\gamma^a\\chi$ and $-\\int(D_a\\bar\\chi)\\gamma^a\\psi=\\int\\bar\\chi\\gamma^aD_a\\psi$; compact support removes both boundary terms. The resulting variation is\n$$i\\int\\bigl(\\bar\\chi\\gamma^aD_a\\psi-(D_a\\bar\\psi)\\gamma^a\\chi\\bigr)d^4x-\\mu\\int(\\bar\\chi\\psi+\\bar\\psi\\chi)d^4x =\\int\\bigl(\\bar\\chi R_D+\\overline{\\bar\\chi R_D}\\bigr)d^4x,$$\nwhere the equality uses $(\\gamma^a)^\\dagger\\beta=\\beta\\gamma^a$ from [F3]. If $R_D(x_0)\\ne0$, continuity and openness of $U$ give a ball contained in $U$ where $|R_D|>0$; choose a bump $\\rho$ from [F4] there and take $\\chi=\\rho\\beta R_D$. Since $\\beta^\\dagger\\beta=I_4$, this gives $\\delta S_D=2\\int\\rho|R_D|^2d^4x>0$, contradicting stationarity. Thus stationarity is equivalent to $R_D=0$ pointwise, and taking the adjoint gives $-i(D_a\\bar\\psi)\\gamma^a-\\mu\\bar\\psi=0$. This is the charged version of the compact-variation identity in [F1]; the additional $a_a$ terms are smooth multiplication terms.",
