              "quote": "Assume Countable Choice [[def-countable-choice]], and use [[def-qft-example-data]] and [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. Write\n$$\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R^3:E=\\sqrt{|\\mathbf q|^2+\\mathsf M^2}\\}$$\nfor the positive shell in that Definition, and $C_+=\\{(E,\\mathbf q)\\in\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^3:E\\geq|\\mathbf q|\\}$ for the closed future cone of $h=(+,-,-,-)$. A proper orthochronous linear $h$-Lorentz map is a real linear $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda^Th\\Lambda=h$, $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and $\\Lambda(C_+)=C_+$. For energy four-vectors on these positive shells, the shell measure $d^3\\mathbf p/((2\\pi)^3\\,2E)$ is invariant under every such map. Let $\\mathsf M_1,\\mathsf M_2\\geq0$, let $P$ be future timelike, meaning $P^0>0$ and $h(P,P)>0$, set $s=h(P,P)>(\\mathsf M_1+\\mathsf M_2)^2$, and write $E_i(r)=\\sqrt{r^2+\\mathsf M_i^2}$. Let $f$ be a nonnegative Borel function on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_1}\\times\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_2}$. For a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda_P$ with $\\Lambda_P P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$, define the finite Borel measure $\\Phi_{2,P}$ as the pushforward of $\\frac{k_*}{16\\pi^2\\sqrt{s}}d\\Omega$ under\n$$\\mathbf n\\longmapsto\\left(\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_1^*,k_*\\mathbf n),\\Lambda_P^{-1}(E_2^*,-k_*\\mathbf n)\\right),$$\nwhere $E_i^*$ and $k_*$ are from [[def-qft-example-two-body-phase-space-data]]. The proof below shows that $\\Phi_{2,P}$ is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda_P$; for $P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$ it is exactly the center-of-momentum measure $d\\Phi_2$ in that Definition. The standard two-body on-shell integral\n$$(2\\pi)^4\\int d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_1}(k_1)d\\nu_{\\mathsf M_2}(k_2)\\, \\delta^{(4)}(P-k_1-k_2)\\,f(k_1,k_2)$$\nis interpreted by first evaluating the spatial delta in $k_2$, then the one-dimensional energy delta by the increasing radial change of variable $r\\mapsto E_1(r)+E_2(r)$. Explicitly, for each fixed angular direction and nonnegative Borel radial function $g:(0,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty]$, the radial action after spatial evaluation is $\\int_0^\\infty g(r)\\delta(\\sqrt{s}-E_1(r)-E_2(r))\\,dr=g(k_*)/(d(E_1+E_2)/dr|_{k_*})$; this is the pushforward evaluation under that $C^1$ diffeomorphism, not a product of distributions. It equals $\\int f\\,d\\Phi_{2,P}$. The threshold root is $k_*$ in that Definition, and\n$$\\int d\\Phi_{2,P}=\\int d\\Phi_2=\\frac{k_*}{4\\pi\\sqrt{s}}.$$\nFor equal rest energies $\\mathsf M_1=\\mathsf M_2=\\mathsf M$, set $\\beta=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/s}$; then $k_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta/2$ and $\\int d\\Phi_{2,P}=\\int d\\Phi_2=\\beta/(8\\pi)$. For the scattering flux identities, take incoming $p_i=(E_i,\\mathbf p_i)\\in\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}}$, where $\\mathsf M_{i,\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$, and require $p_1+p_2=P$. A center-of-momentum map is a proper orthochronous $h$-Lorentz map $\\Lambda$ with $\\Lambda P=(\\sqrt{s},\\mathbf0)$; write $\\Lambda p_1=(E_1^*,\\mathbf p_1^*)$ and define $p_*=|\\mathbf p_1^*|$. The value is independent of the choice of $\\Lambda$, since two such maps differ by a spatial rotation. Their invariant flux is $F=4p_*\\sqrt{s}$. For equal incoming rest energies $\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}\\geq0$ with $s\\geq4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2$, define $\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}=\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M_{\\mathrm{in}}^2/s}$; then $p_*=\\sqrt{s}\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}/2$ and $F=2s\\beta_{\\mathrm{in}}$. For finite nonempty incoming and outgoing label sets, normalized initial weights, and the Borel amplitude family of the Definition, whenever $F>0$ the cross-section coefficient map gives\n$$\\frac{d\\sigma_{\\mathrm{coeff}}}{d\\Omega} =\\frac{(\\hbar c)^2}{64\\pi^2s}\\, \\frac{k_*}{p_*}\\, \\frac{\\overline{|\\mathcal A|^2}}{S_f}.$$\nFor a parent at rest, represented by $P_0=(\\mathsf M_0,\\mathbf0)$ with $\\mathsf M_0>2\\mathsf M$, and two equal-rest-energy daughters on $\\mathcal H^+_{\\mathsf M}$ with total four-vector $P_0$ and constant amplitude $G_E$ in joules, the rate coefficient is $|G_E|^2\\sqrt{1-4\\mathsf M^2/\\mathsf M_0^2}/(32\\pi\\hbar\\mathsf M_0)$ when the daughters are identical. This is a coefficient calculation, not a claim of exponential survival.",
