              "claim": "By [F5]\u2013[F6], $P_n$ is an orthogonal projection and $I-P_n$ is its orthogonal complement. The two Kraus operators $P_n$ and $I-P_n$ are bounded and satisfy $P_n^*P_n+(I-P_n)^*(I-P_n)=P_n+(I-P_n)=I$, so they form a normalized finite projective instrument. The state from step 3.1 is a unit vector. Put $\\rho_{\\lambda,\\Lambda}:=T_{\\Psi_{\\lambda,\\Lambda},\\Psi_{\\lambda,\\Lambda}}$, with $T_{\\Psi,\\Psi}x=\\Psi\\langle\\Psi,x\\rangle_2$; by the unit-vector case in [A3], this is the density-operator input to the Born postulate. Applying its outcome formula to the first Kraus operator $P_n$ gives $p_n=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho_{\\lambda,\\Lambda}P_n)=\\|P_n\\Psi_{\\lambda,\\Lambda}\\|^2$. This uses the finite two-outcome partition for each singleton event and assumes no countably infinite Kraus instrument.",
