)-(c'+d')=(c-c')+(d-d')\\in N$; and for any scalar $t$, $tc-tc'=t(c-c')\\in N$. Therefore $[c]+[d]:=[c+d]$ and $t[c]:=[tc]$ do not depend on representatives. The quotient map $c\\mapsto[c]$ preserves zero, addition and scalar multiplication; applying it to each vector-space identity in $F$ proves all vector-space axioms in $F/N$. Every generator in $\\mathcal R$ lies in $N$, so its class is zero and the two displayed relations hold. [F1, F2, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎",
