        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. The physical energy of $x\\in\\Omega$ is $E(x)=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$, with its zero and any retained internal offsets fixed by the declared material model. Energies are evaluated in the center-of-mass rest frame specified by [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]], and $U$ is the corresponding macroscopic internal-energy coordinate of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Equal energy values at distinct elements of $\\Omega$ count as distinct microstates.\n\nWhen two such models are declared independent, their joint microstate set is the Cartesian product $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$, and additivity of the microscopic energy is the explicit condition $\\epsilon_{AB}(x,y)=\\epsilon_A(x)+\\epsilon_B(y)$. Independence and this energy equation are model data; they do not follow merely from the two systems being separated. The definition itself assigns no weights, temperature, probability law, or dynamics.",
