        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assign a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins and the exact framework constant $k_{\\mathrm B}$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$ from [[def-td-si-quantities]], and put $\\beta=(k_{\\mathrm B}T)^{-1}$ in $\\mathrm{J^{-1}}$. For a finite microstate model $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$, adopt the canonical preparation\n$$P_\\beta(x)=\\frac{e^{-\\beta E(x)}}{Z(\\beta)},\\qquad Z(\\beta)=\\sum_{x\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E(x)}.$$\nHere $Z$ is dimensionless, positive, and finite because $\\Omega$ is finite and nonempty.\n\nThe same adopted law may be assigned to a specified countable state model with its stated enumeration and energy list, or to a measurable model $(X,\\Sigma,\\mu,E)$ with a dimensionless reference measure $\\mu$ and measurable energy $E:X\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$, only when $0<Z(\\beta)<\\infty$ for the declared series or integral. In those cases $P_\\beta(i)=e^{-\\beta E_i}/Z$ or the probability density is $p_\\beta=e^{-\\beta E}/Z$ relative to $\\mu$. The countable and integral conventions are those of [[def-tdmath-countable-canonical-model]] and [[def-tdmath-measure-integral]] at their actual current draft statements.\n\nThis is an adopted statistical-mechanical preparation rule. It is not derived from the macroscopic thermodynamic laws, a finite bath, entropy maximization, or a time-evolution equation. It does not assert that a physical sample has this preparation; that identification is an additional model assumption to be tested against a specified preparation and apparatus.",
