      },
      {
        "id": "thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain",
        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "GNS Hilbert reconstruction with invariant operator domain",
        "reference": "complete-local-arguments.md#C1",
        "design_state": "Fully authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 2,
        "deps": [
          "lem-qft-positive-state-cauchy-schwarz",
          "thm-completion-of-an-inner-product-space-is-hilbert"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "lem-qft-positive-state-cauchy-schwarz": "mathematical-premise",
          "thm-completion-of-an-inner-product-space-is-hilbert": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $A$ be a complex vector space with a complex-bilinear associative product, unit $1_A$, and conjugate-linear involution satisfying $(ab)^*=b^*a^*$, $(a^*)^*=a$ and $1_A^*=1_A$. Let $\\omega:A\\to\\mathbb C$ be complex-linear with $\\omega(1_A)=1$ and real nonnegative $\\omega(a^*a)$ for every $a\\in A$. Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$: every countable family of nonempty sets has a choice function. Put $N_\\omega:=\\{a\\in A:\\omega(a^*a)=0\\}$ and $\\mathcal D_\\omega:=A/N_\\omega$, with\n\n$$([a],[b])_\\omega:=\\omega(b^*a),$$\n\nusing the first-variable-linear convention. Let $H_\\omega$ be the Hilbert completion of this inner-product space, and identify $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ with its canonical dense image in $H_\\omega$. For each $a\\in A$ define left multiplication on the common domain $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ by $\\pi_\\omega(a)[b]:=[ab]$. Then $\\pi_\\omega$ is a unital algebra representation of $A$ on the invariant dense domain $\\mathcal D_\\omega$, and\n\n$$\\mathcal D_\\omega\\subseteq D(\\pi_\\omega(a)^*),\\qquad \\pi_\\omega(a)^*|_{\\mathcal D_\\omega}=\\pi_\\omega(a^*).$$\n\nEach $\\pi_\\omega(a)$ is closable, meaning that the closure of its graph in $H_\\omega\\times H_\\omega$ is the graph of an operator. The vector $\\Omega_\\omega:=[1_A]$ is a unit cyclic vector, $\\pi_\\omega(A)\\Omega_\\omega=\\mathcal D_\\omega$, and $\\omega(a)=(\\pi_\\omega(a)\\Omega_\\omega,\\Omega_\\omega)$. For uniqueness, let $K$ be a Hilbert space, $\\mathcal D'\\subseteq K$ a dense linear subspace invariant under every operator $\\rho(a)$, and $\\rho:A\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathcal D')$ a unital algebra representation satisfying $(\\rho(a)x,y)=(x,\\rho(a^*)y)$ for all $a\\in A$ and $x,y\\in\\mathcal D'$. If $\\Omega'\\in\\mathcal D'$, $\\mathcal D'=\\rho(A)\\Omega'$, and $\\omega(a)=(\\rho(a)\\Omega',\\Omega')$, then there is a unique unitary $V:H_\\omega\\to K$ with $V\\Omega_\\omega=\\Omega'$ and $V\\pi_\\omega(a)x=\\rho(a)Vx$ for all $a\\in A$ and $x\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$. This theorem supplies neither a C*-norm nor essential self-adjointness of $\\pi_\\omega(a)$ when $a=a^*$.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 By [F1], $N_\\omega$ is a subspace and every element of it pairs to zero with every element of $A$. Thus $([a],[b])_\\omega=\\omega(b^*a)$ is independent of both representatives; it is Hermitian and nonnegative, and $([a],[a])_\\omega=0$ holds exactly when $a\\in N_\\omega$, so the quotient pairing is positive definite. [F1, given]\n\n1.2 For $a,b\\in A$, replacing $b$ by $b+n$ with $n\\in N_\\omega$ changes $ab$ by $an\\in N_\\omega$ because $N_\\omega$ is a left ideal by [F1]; hence $\\pi_\\omega(a)[b]=[ab]$ is well defined. Bilinearity, associativity and $1_A$ give $\\pi_\\omega(a+b)=\\pi_\\omega(a)+\\pi_\\omega(b)$, $\\pi_\\omega(\\lambda a)=\\lambda\\pi_\\omega(a)$, $\\pi_\\omega(ab)=\\pi_\\omega(a)\\pi_\\omega(b)$ and $\\pi_\\omega(1_A)=I_{\\mathcal D_\\omega}$, and left multiplication keeps $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ invariant. [F1, given, algebra]\n\n2.1 Apply [F2] to the quotient inner-product space $\\mathcal D_\\omega$; under the stated Countable Choice assumption this gives the Hilbert completion $H_\\omega$ and a dense isometric embedding of $\\mathcal D_\\omega$, which we identify with its image. This is the only completion invoked. [F2, given, step 1.1]\n\n2.2 For $a,b,c\\in A$, the quotient form gives $(\\pi_\\omega(a)[b],[c])_\\omega=\\omega(c^*ab)=([b],\\pi_\\omega(a^*)[c])_\\omega$. By the definition of the Hilbert-space adjoint, this identity for all $[b]\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$ shows $[c]\\in D(\\pi_\\omega(a)^*)$ and $\\pi_\\omega(a)^*[c]=\\pi_\\omega(a^*)[c]$. [step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra]\n\n2.3 Since $([1_A],[1_A])_\\omega=\\omega(1_A)=1$, $\\Omega_\\omega$ is a unit vector. Also $\\pi_\\omega(a)\\Omega_\\omega=[a]$ for every $a\\in A$, so its algebraic orbit is exactly $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ and is dense. Finally $(\\pi_\\omega(a)\\Omega_\\omega,\\Omega_\\omega)_\\omega=([a],[1_A])_\\omega=\\omega(a)$, recovering the state. [given, step 1.1, step 1.2]\n\n2.4 Let $(H',\\mathcal D',\\rho,\\Omega')$ be another algebraically cyclic *-representation of $A$ with common invariant dense domain $\\mathcal D'=\\rho(A)\\Omega'$, $\\rho(a^*)=\\rho(a)^*|_{\\mathcal D'}$, and $\\omega(a)=(\\rho(a)\\Omega',\\Omega')$. Define $U[a]:=\\rho(a)\\Omega'$. If $[a]=[0]$, then $\\|\\rho(a)\\Omega'\\|^2=\\omega(a^*a)=0$, so $U$ is well defined; and for $a,b\\in A$, $(U[a],U[b])=\\omega(b^*a)=([a],[b])_\\omega$ by the representation's adjoint identity. Thus $U$ is an isometry from $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ onto the dense subspace $\\mathcal D'$. [given, F1, step 1.1, step 1.2]\n\n3.1 Suppose $x_n\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$, $x_n\\to0$ in $H_\\omega$, and $\\pi_\\omega(a)x_n\\to y$ in $H_\\omega$. For each $z\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$, step 2.2 gives $(y,z)_\\omega=\\lim_n(\\pi_\\omega(a)x_n,z)_\\omega=\\lim_n(x_n,\\pi_\\omega(a^*)z)_\\omega=0$. Density forces $y=0$. Therefore the closed graph relation of $\\pi_\\omega(a)$ contains no two distinct outputs over one input: their difference would be a nonzero pair $(0,y)$. The graph closure is a graph, so $\\pi_\\omega(a)$ is closable. In particular, if $a=a^*$ then $\\pi_\\omega(a)$ is symmetric on $\\mathcal D_\\omega$; this does not prove essential self-adjointness. [step 2.2, given]\n\n4.1 The isometry extends uniquely from the dense domain to a unitary $\\overline U:H_\\omega\\to H'$ because its range $\\mathcal D'$ is dense. It sends $\\Omega_\\omega$ to $\\Omega'$ and, for $[b]\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$, $\\overline U\\pi_\\omega(a)[b]=\\rho(ab)\\Omega'=\\rho(a)\\overline U[b]$; hence it intertwines the representations on the common domains. Uniqueness follows because every vector of $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ has the form $\\pi_\\omega(a)\\Omega_\\omega$. [step 2.3, step 2.4, step 1.2] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
