      {
        "id": "lem-qft-state-symmetry-implementer",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "State-preserving symmetries implemented unitarily",
        "reference": "complete-local-arguments.md#C2",
        "design_state": "Fully authored draft with complete local argument; independent review remains pending.",
        "dependency_level": 3,
        "deps": [
          "thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Assume Countable Choice $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, the hypothesis of [[thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain]]. Let $G$ be a topological group with identity $e$. Let $A$ be a unital complex star algebra with positive normalized state $\\omega$, and let $\\alpha:G\\to\\operatorname{Aut}^*(A)$ be a group action: each $\\alpha_g$ is a bijective complex-linear map preserving product, unit and involution, with $\\alpha_e=\\mathrm{id}$ and $\\alpha_g\\alpha_h=\\alpha_{gh}$. Suppose $\\omega(\\alpha_g(a))=\\omega(a)$ for all $g\\in G$ and $a\\in A$. Also suppose that, for every $a,b\\in A$, the coefficient function $g\\mapsto\\omega(b^*\\alpha_g(a))$ is continuous at $e$.\n\nUse the GNS representation from [[thm-qft-algebraic-gns-domain]], with common dense domain $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ and cyclic vector $\\Omega_\\omega$. Then $U_g[a]:=[\\alpha_g(a)]$ extends to a strongly continuous unitary representation $U:G\\to\\mathcal U(H_\\omega)$, fixes $\\Omega_\\omega$, and satisfies\n\n$$U_g\\pi_\\omega(a)U_g^{-1}|_{\\mathcal D_\\omega}=\\pi_\\omega(\\alpha_g(a))\\qquad(a\\in A,g\\in G).$$\n\nHere strongly continuous means that $g\\mapsto U_g\\xi$ is norm-continuous for every $\\xi\\in H_\\omega$. No differentiability, generator domain or spectral theorem follows from these hypotheses alone.",
        "strategy": "**Proof technique:** direct.\n\n1.1 If $n\\in N_\\omega$, then $\\omega((\\alpha_g n)^*\\alpha_g n)=\\omega(\\alpha_g(n^*n))=\\omega(n^*n)=0$, so $\\alpha_g(N_\\omega)\\subseteq N_\\omega$. Applying the same argument to $g^{-1}$ gives equality. For $a,b\\in A$, $([\\alpha_g(a)],[\\alpha_g(b)])_\\omega=\\omega(\\alpha_g(b^*a))=\\omega(b^*a)$, so $[a]\\mapsto[\\alpha_g(a)]$ is an isometry on $\\mathcal D_\\omega$ with inverse induced by $\\alpha_{g^{-1}}$; it extends uniquely to a unitary $U_g$ on $H_\\omega$. The action law gives $U_gU_h=U_{gh}$, and $U_g\\Omega_\\omega=\\Omega_\\omega$. [F1, A1, given]\n\n2.1 For $[b]\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$, $U_g\\pi_\\omega(a)U_g^{-1}[b]=U_g[a\\alpha_{g^{-1}}(b)]=[\\alpha_g(a)b]=\\pi_\\omega(\\alpha_g(a))[b]$, using the representation rule and multiplicativity of $\\alpha_g$. This is the claimed covariance identity on the common domain. [F1, A1, step 1.1]\n\n2.2 For $a\\in A$, state invariance and the quotient inner product give $\\|U_g[a]-[a]\\|^2=2\\omega(a^*a)-\\omega(a^*\\alpha_g(a))-\\omega((\\alpha_g(a))^*a)$. The first cross term tends to $\\omega(a^*a)$ as $g\\to e$ by [A2]. For the second, invariance gives $\\omega((\\alpha_g(a))^*a)=\\omega(\\alpha_{g^{-1}}((\\alpha_g(a))^*a))=\\omega(a^*\\alpha_{g^{-1}}(a))$, which also tends to $\\omega(a^*a)$ by [A2] and continuity of inversion. Thus $U_g[a]\\to[a]$ for every $[a]\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$. [F1, A1, A2, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Let $\\xi\\in H_\\omega$ and $\\varepsilon>0$. Choose $y\\in\\mathcal D_\\omega$ with $\\|\\xi-y\\|<\\varepsilon/3$. Since each $U_g$ is unitary, $\\|U_g\\xi-\\xi\\|\\le2\\|\\xi-y\\|+\\|U_gy-y\\|$; step 2.2 makes the last term less than $\\varepsilon/3$ for $g$ sufficiently near $e$. Hence every orbit is continuous at $e$. For any $g_0\\in G$, $\\|U_g\\xi-U_{g_0}\\xi\\|=\\|U_{g_0^{-1}g}\\xi-\\xi\\|$, so continuity of multiplication transfers continuity to $g_0$. The unitary representation is strongly continuous on all of $G$. [step 1.1, step 2.2, given, algebra] ∎",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Konrad Schmüdgen, An Invitation to Unbounded Representations of *-Algebras on Hilbert Space, Graduate Texts in Mathematics 285, Springer Nature (2020), §4.4",
              "url": "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-46366-3",
