        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "Let $I$ be a finite nonempty set of system types, $X_i$ a nonempty state set with chosen reference $r_i\\in X_i$ for each $i\\in I$, and $M$ a common meter state set with $z_0,z_1\\in M$. Assume for every $i$ that the product state set $X_i\\times M$ satisfies the hypotheses of [[thm-tdmath-additive-entropy-representation]], with strict reference pair $(r_i,z_0)\\prec\\prec(r_i,z_1)$. Let $S_i$ be its normalized representation and put $\\sigma_i(x):=S_i(x,z_0)$. In that product comparison, the unit product-state point mass satisfies $e_{(x,z_0)}\\sim_s(1-\\sigma_i(x))e_{(r_i,z_0)}+\\sigma_i(x)e_{(r_i,z_1)}$, by [[lem-tdmath-reference-supremum-bounds]]. These product-state point masses are distinct from the shared state-and-meter compounds defined next.\n\nAssume in addition a **common-meter compatibility interface**. Let $\\mathcal Y$ contain disjoint families of raw state tags $a_{i,x}$, raw type-reference tags $\\rho_i$, and two distinct raw meter tags $\\zeta_0,\\zeta_1$. Using the unit point masses on these tags, write $e_{r_i}:=e_{\\rho_i}$ and $e_{z_j}:=e_{\\zeta_j}$, and define $u_{i,x}:=e_{a_{i,x}}+e_{z_0}$. Thus $u_{i,x}$ is an explicit mass-two state-and-fixed-meter compound; it is not a unit tag. Compounds over $\\mathcal Y$ use the finite-support convention of [[def-tdmath-tagged-scalable-compound]], and their signed comparison is the one in [[def-tdmath-signed-comparison]]. Require the exact substitution\n\n$$u_{i,x}\\sim_s e_{r_i}+(1-\\sigma_i(x))e_{z_0}+\\sigma_i(x)e_{z_1}\\qquad(i\\in I,\\ x\\in X_i),$$\n\nfinite substitutions to compose, and the fixed reference sum $\\sum_iT_ie_{r_i}$ to cancel when type totals agree. Both sides of this signed substitution have total coefficient two, even when $\\sigma_i(x)$ lies outside $[0,1]$. Require the common meter point masses to satisfy $e_{z_0}\\preceq e_{z_1}$ and not $e_{z_1}\\preceq e_{z_0}$. These are hypotheses on the comparison algebra, not consequences of separate numerical meter readings.\n\nFor positive coefficients $t_{ik}$ on a finite collection of type-$i$ states $x_{ik}$, define $T_i:=\\sum_k t_{ik}$ and $T:=\\sum_iT_i$. Put $C_0:=\\sum_{i,k}t_{ik}e_{a_{i,x_{ik}}}$ and $C:=\\sum_{i,k}t_{ik}u_{i,x_{ik}}=C_0+T e_{z_0}$. Thus $C_0$ is the state compound of mass $T$ and $C$ is its fixed-meter encoding of mass $2T$. Empty collections give $T=0$ and $C_0=C=0$. Define\n\n$$\\Sigma(C):=\\sum_{i\\in I}\\sum_k t_{ik}\\sigma_i(x_{ik}).$$\n\nFor two such compounds $C,D$ with the same type-total vector $(T_i)_{i\\in I}$, the common comparison satisfies $C\\preceq D$ exactly when $\\Sigma(C)\\le\\Sigma(D)$. Equivalently, the same order criterion holds for the state compounds $C_0,D_0$ after canceling their common $T e_{z_0}$. The index family is finite; no arbitrary-index choice or infinite decomposition is asserted.",
        "strategy": "Proof strategy: direct\n\n**Given:** [A1] and [F1]–[F3].\n\n1.1 For each constituent $(i,x_{ik},z_0)$ of $C$, its common-algebra token $u_{i,x_{ik}}$ is equivalent by [F1] and the exact identification in [A1] to $e_{r_i}+(1-\\sigma_i(x_{ik}))e_{z_0}+\\sigma_i(x_{ik})e_{z_1}$. Scale by $t_{ik}$ and combine the finitely many comparisons using [A1] and [F2]. The resulting reference-system part is $\\sum_iT_i e_{r_i}$, independent of the chosen states. The meter part has coefficients $T-\\Sigma(C)$ on $e_{z_0}$ and $\\Sigma(C)$ on $e_{z_1}$, where $T:=\\sum_iT_i$. Hence $C$ is equivalent to that fixed reference-system compound plus $(T-\\Sigma(C))e_{z_0}+\\Sigma(C)e_{z_1}$. [A1, F1, F2, algebra]\n\n2.1 The same substitution for $D$ gives the same reference-system compound because its type totals are the same, and the same total meter mass $T$. Cancel the common reference-system terms by [A1] and [F2]. The remaining comparison is between equal-mass meter expressions whose $e_{z_1}$ coefficients are $\\Sigma(C)$ and $\\Sigma(D)$; [F3] says it holds exactly when $\\Sigma(C)\\le\\Sigma(D)$. Reversing the same equivalences proves the converse implication as well. [A1, F2, F3, step 1.1] ∎",
