        "id": "def-td-reservoir-cycle",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Ideal-reservoir thermodynamic cycles",
        "statement": "Fix a working system $s$ of constitution $c$ and a closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ with equilibrium endpoints as in [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. A cycle of the working system is such a record with $x_P^-=x_P^+$; the reservoirs and work store need not return to their initial energy states.\n\nAn ideal thermal reservoir $b$ has an energy interval $J_b\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ measured in joules and a finite entropy function $S_b:J_b\\to\\mathbb R$ measured in $\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$. On the energy interval admitted for a cycle, assume the exact affine law\n$\nS_b(E_b)=s_{b,0}+\\frac{E_b}{T_b}\n$\nfor a constant $s_{b,0}$ and fixed $T_b>0$ kelvins. Thus $S_b'(E_b)=1/T_b$ in the interior and every admitted endpoint change is exactly $\\Delta S_b=\\Delta E_b/T_b$. This constant is the absolute thermodynamic temperature under [[def-td-temperature-pressure-chemical-potential]]. The affine entropy law on the admitted interval is the ideal-bath model; a finite reservoir whose temperature changes is outside this definition.\n\nAn ideal work store is a named auxiliary with an energy coordinate $E_w$ measured in joules and an assigned entropy $S_w(E_w)=s_w$ constant on the energy interval used by the cycle. It exchanges only mechanical work and no matter or heat. Its energy may change, while its entropy change is zero by this model definition.\n\nA two-reservoir cycle contains one working-system cycle, one hot bath at $T_h$, one cold bath at $T_c$, and the ideal work store. They lie inside one isolated outer boundary in the sense of [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]], so the full compound has no external matter or energy transfer. There are no unlisted heat, work, matter, kinetic-energy, or external-potential-energy changes. The admitted process and transfer signs are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The model declares the endpoint entropy split\n$\nS_{\\mathrm{tot}}=S_{\\mathrm{ws}}(x_{\\mathrm{ws}})+S_h(E_h)+S_{\\mathrm{cold}}(E_c)+S_w(E_w),\n$\nwhere $S_{\\mathrm{ws}}$ is the working-system entropy and the bath and work-store functions are those defined here. This additive constituent split is assumed to be one admitted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] and is the entropy supplied to [[post-td-macroscopic-second-law]]; $S_w$ contributes zero change. Reversibility of the full record is as in [[def-td-reversible-process]].\n\nWhen the second law is applied to the cycle, the model also supplies an admitted closed process record for the full compound. At both endpoints its constituent preparations, with the declared contact controls locked or insulated, are an equilibrium macrostate for that record; its separately tracked center-of-mass kinetic and external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change. This total-compound record is the input to the second law; the working-system record $P$ is the input to its first-law balance.\n\nIn the heat-engine mode, the positive magnitudes $Q_h,Q_c$ in joules mean respectively heat taken from the hot bath into the working system and heat rejected by the working system to the cold bath; accordingly $\\Delta E_h=-Q_h$, $\\Delta E_c=Q_c$, $Q[P]=Q_h-Q_c$, and work output is $W_{\\mathrm{out}}=-W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]>0$. The work store gains exactly this transferred work, so $\\Delta E_w=W_{\\mathrm{out}}$. In the refrigerator mode, $Q_c>0$ is heat taken from the cold bath into the working system, $Q_h>0$ is heat rejected to the hot bath, so $\\Delta E_c=-Q_c$, $\\Delta E_h=Q_h$, $Q[P]=Q_c-Q_h$, and work input is $W_{\\mathrm{in}}=W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P]>0$. The store supplies that work, so $\\Delta E_w=-W_{\\mathrm{in}}$. These are idealized model conventions, not claims that a particular apparatus realizes the cycle.",
