        "kind": "remark",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Residual entropy and the scope of the third-law postulates",
        "statement": "Residual entropy here means a finite entropy limit at low temperature under a model's specified entropy convention. In a finite canonical model, let a finite set of microstates have energies $E_i$ in joules, assign $p_i(T)\\propto\\exp[-E_i/(k_{\\mathrm B}T)]$ for $T>0$, and use the Gibbs entropy $-k_{\\mathrm B}\\sum_i p_i(T)\\log p_i(T)$. If the ground energy has finite degeneracy $g$ and there is a positive gap to all excited states, this model-specific entropy tends to $k_{\\mathrm B}\\log g$; [[ex-td-finite-canonical-ground-state-entropy]] supplies the full finite-state calculation. Here $g$ is the number of ground states and $\\log$ is the natural logarithm of [[def-natural-logarithm]].\n\nThis formula belongs to that specified microscopic preparation and entropy convention. It does not identify every Gibbs entropy with the macroscopic $S_c$, and it does not establish a universal value $S_c(0)=0$. The uniform Nernst postulate [[post-td-nernst-scoped]] constrains differences across its declared family, not the common entropy offset: adding one fixed constant to every $S_K(t,a)$ leaves every displayed difference unchanged. Thus that postulate alone neither selects an absolute zero nor forces a unique ground state. The Nernst heat theorem, zero-entropy convention for a unique ground state, and operational unattainability remain distinct model statements.",
