        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "statement": "A finite statistical microstate model is a pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ where $\\Omega$ is a finite nonempty set and $\\epsilon:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ assigns a finite numerical energy to each microstate. The physical energy of $x\\in\\Omega$ is $E(x)=\\epsilon(x)\\,\\mathrm J$, with its zero and any retained internal offsets fixed by the declared material model. For a physical internal-energy interpretation, the declared companion mechanical model supplies, for every admitted microstate preparation $x$, a positive total mass and a center-of-mass position map with a defined componentwise velocity at that preparation, obeying the Galilean transformation conditions of [[def-td-galilean-rest-frame]]. That velocity selects the statewise instantaneous rest chart in which $E(x)$ is recorded; a finite set and energy map alone do not construct these mechanical data or a common rest chart for a time interval. Bulk translational kinetic energy and separately tracked external-field energy are excluded from these internal-energy values. The energy zero and any internal offsets are fixed consistently across admitted states. If the statistical model also assigns a single equilibrium macrostate to a preparation, $U$ is its internal-energy coordinate of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]; the pair $(\\Omega,\\epsilon)$ alone does not assert that macroscopic identification. Equal energy values at distinct elements of $\\Omega$ count as distinct microstates.\n\nWhen two such models are declared independent, their joint microstate set is the Cartesian product $\\Omega_A\\times\\Omega_B$, and additivity of the microscopic energy is the explicit condition $\\epsilon_{AB}(x,y)=\\epsilon_A(x)+\\epsilon_B(y)$. Independence and this energy equation are model data; they do not follow merely from the two systems being separated. The definition itself assigns no weights, temperature, probability law, or dynamics.",
