P<\\infty$, its expectation is the integral $\\mathbb E_P[\\Sigma]:=\\int_\\Gamma\\Sigma\\,dP$ by [[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]. A driving protocol is a declared control map $\\lambda:[0,\\tau]\\to\\Lambda$, with its parameter space $\\Lambda$, regularity and endpoint controls specified by the model. Any reverse-protocol comparison needs its own path measure and support conditions.\n\nThe fluctuation–dissipation theorem discussed in the cited treatments relates equilibrium fluctuations to a specified linear response of a coupled model. It is distinct from a trajectory fluctuation relation and does not by itself prove the macroscopic second-law postulate. No universal microscopic fluctuation formula or trajectory-wise entropy inequality is asserted here.",
