        "physical_scope": "A finite-microstate, finite-stage, single-reservoir protocol model with externally changed energies and discrete bath transitions; it does not assert that a material apparatus has this dynamics.",
        "statement": "Take $\\Omega$ to be a finite nonempty microstate set as in [[def-td-finite-microstate-model]]: distinct elements count as distinct microstates, and the physical energy values are finite and measured in joules in the declared rest frame. Fix an integer $m\\ge1$ and stage energy functions $E_k:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R\\,\\mathrm J$ for $k=0,\\ldots,m$, with the energy offsets fixed by the declared protocol model. Fix inverse temperature $\\beta>0$ in joules$^{-1}$. The finite partition sum and canonical probability vector at stage $k$ are $Z_k=\\sum_{i\\in\\Omega}e^{-\\beta E_k(i)}$ and $\\pi_k(i)=e^{-\\beta E_k(i)}/Z_k$. Since $\\Omega$ is nonempty and finite and each energy is finite, $0<Z_k<\\infty$ and $\\pi_k$ is a strictly positive probability vector.\n\nA transition kernel at stage $k$ is a matrix $K_k(i,j)\\ge0$ with $\\sum_{j\\in\\Omega}K_k(i,j)=1$. In the forward stage order, the energy changes instantaneously from $E_{k-1}$ to $E_k$ while the state is still $i_{k-1}$; the subsequent bath transition samples $i_k$ using $K_k(i_{k-1},i_k)$ at fixed energy $E_k$. A path is $\\omega=(i_0,\\ldots,i_m)\\in\\Omega^{m+1}$. For an initial law $\\mu_0$, its path probability is $P_F(\\omega)=\\mu_0(i_0)\\prod_{k=1}^mK_k(i_{k-1},i_k)$.\n\nWork on the system is positive when energy is supplied to it, and heat into the system is positive when a bath transition raises its energy. Define $W(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=1}^m[E_k(i_{k-1})-E_{k-1}(i_{k-1})]$ and $Q_{\\mathrm{in}}(\\omega)=\\sum_{k=1}^m[E_k(i_k)-E_k(i_{k-1})]$, both in joules. Define $F_k=-\\beta^{-1}\\log Z_k$ and $\\Delta F=F_m-F_0=-\\beta^{-1}\\log(Z_m/Z_0)$. These are finite-model quantities; their physical interpretation is conditional on the adopted preparation and transition assumptions."
