        "physical_scope": "Finite-state, time-homogeneous continuous-time rate models and their reversible discrete transition-kernel analogue; the definition does not assert that a physical system follows either model.",
        "statement": "A finite rate model consists of a finite nonempty state set $S$ and rates $q_{ij}\\ge0$ in seconds$^{-1}$ for $i\\ne j$. Set $q_{ii}=-\\sum_{j\\ne i}q_{ij}$. A row probability vector is $p=(p_i)_{i\\in S}$ with $p_i\\ge0$ and $\\sum_i p_i=1$. Its time-dependent law $p(t)$ follows the continuous-time master equation $\\dot p_i=\\sum_{j\\ne i}[p_jq_{ji}-p_iq_{ij}]$. A strictly positive normalized vector $\\pi$ is a detailed-balance law when $\\pi_iq_{ij}=\\pi_jq_{ji}$ for every distinct pair. Its undirected rate graph has an edge $\\{i,j\\}$ exactly when $\\pi_iq_{ij}>0$; detailed balance makes this equivalent to $\\pi_jq_{ji}>0$.\n\nFor a discrete transition kernel $K$ on a finite state set, $\\pi$-detailed balance means $\\pi_iK(i,j)=\\pi_jK(j,i)$ for every pair. If $\\pi$ is strictly positive, this identity pairs the supports of forward and reversed transitions. A rate model or a reversible kernel is a mathematical/physical model class; no observed trajectory is asserted by the definition."
