        "statement": "For a $C^2$ future-directed timelike worldline $x:I\\to M$ on a prescribed smooth, time-oriented Lorentzian background $(M,g,T)$ of signature $(-+++)$, with $m>0$ a fixed primitive mass in kg and $c>0$ in m/s, and any compact segment $[a,b]\\subset I$ with $a<b$, adopt the stationary-action model on that segment\n\n$$S_{m,[a,b]}[x]=-mc\\int_a^b\\sqrt{-g(x',x') }\\,d\\lambda.$$\n\nHere $x$ is $C^2$ on a neighborhood of $[a,b]$ (so its tangent is $C^1$ there), and $\\lambda$ is an increasing regular parameter. An admissible variation is a one-parameter family $x_\\epsilon(\\lambda)$ that is $C^2$ jointly in $(\\epsilon,\\lambda)$, has fixed endpoint events $x_\\epsilon(a)=x(a)$ and $x_\\epsilon(b)=x(b)$, and remains future timelike for sufficiently small $|\\epsilon|$. The integrand is the proper-time length density, so $S_{m,[a,b]}=-mc^2\\int_a^b d\\tau$; its dimension is action (J s). The adopted premise concerns this ideal free test body only. It is neither a theorem about all matter nor an empirical report, and supplies no backreaction or completeness claim. The flat ideal-clock specialization agrees with [[post-sr-ideal-clocks-and-light]]; adopting this action on a curved background remains a separate premise.\n\nThe cotangent phase-space carrier convention assumes countable choice ACω only; full choice is not assumed.",
