        "statement": "In affine Minkowski space with $x^0=ct$, let $m\\ge0$, $p_i$ be spatial momentum components and $p_i\\ne0$ for $m=0$. On the future shell, $p_0=-E/c$, where\n\n$$E(\\mathbf p)=\\sqrt{m^2c^4+c^2|\\mathbf p|^2}.$$\n\nOn any compact interval $J=[t_1,t_2]$ of inertial time with $t_1<t_2$, the gauge $x^0=ct$ reduces the first-order shell action to\n\n$$I_J=\\int_{t_1}^{t_2}\\left(p_i\\dot q^i-E(\\mathbf p)\\right)dt,\\qquad q^i=x^i.$$\n\nFor this variational statement, take $q^i$ and $p_i$ smooth on a neighborhood of $J$ and restrict to variations that are smooth one-parameter families, fix $q$ at both endpoints, and vary $p$ freely; in the massless case the paths and sufficiently small variations stay in $\\mathbf p\\ne0$. The resulting Hamilton equations hold on the interior of $J$.\n\nThe gauge pair $C_m=0$, $\\psi=x^0-ct=0$ is second class because $\\{C_m,\\psi\\}=-P^0\\ne0$ on the future shell; its Dirac reduction gives the canonical spatial bracket. The reduced Hamilton equations are $\\dot q^i=c^2p_i/E$, $\\dot p_i=0$, agreeing with the unreduced characteristic trajectories. On this full flat future shell every characteristic line meets each inertial-time slice once; this global assertion does not extend by this proof to a curved temporal chart.",
