        "title": "Local Hamilton-Jacobi solution by invertible characteristics",
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $H\\in C^\\infty(U,\\mathbb R)$ on an open set $U\\subset\\mathbb R\\times T^*\\mathbb R^n$, with coordinates $(t,q,p)$, and let $S_0\\in C^\\infty(Q_0,\\mathbb R)$ on an open $Q_0\\subset\\mathbb R^n$. The Hamilton characteristic ODE is $\\dot q^i=\\partial H/\\partial p_i$ and $\\dot p_i=-\\partial H/\\partial q^i$. For a covector $p$ and vector $v$ in the canonical coordinates, write $p\\cdot v:=\\sum_i p_i v^i$. For each $a_0\\in Q_0$ such that $(0,a_0,dS_0(a_0))\\in U$, there are neighbourhoods of $(0,a_0)$ and a time interval on which this ODE with $q(0,a)=a$, $p(0,a)=dS_0(a)$ has a smooth solution and $(t,a)\\mapsto(t,q(t,a))$ is a diffeomorphism onto its image after restriction. On that image the function\n\n$$S(t,q(t,a))=S_0(a)+\\int_0^t[p(u,a)\\cdot\\dot q(u,a)-H(u,q(u,a),p(u,a))],du$$\n\nis smooth, has initial value $S_0$, and satisfies $S_t+H(t,q,S_q)=0$. The claim is local to the invertible projection domain; it asserts no continuation after a caustic. $S$ has action units, so $[S]=\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $[S_q]=[p]$, and $[S_t]=\\mathrm J$ when $t,q,p,H$ use SI time, length, momentum, and energy units.",
