        "kind": "theorem",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "Positive classical spin Poincare orbit and stabilizer jump",
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. Let $G=\\mathbb R^4\\rtimes SO^+(1,3)$ act coadjointly on the dual charge space of [[def-rpm-h-poincare-charge-space]]. At a charge point $(P^a,J^{ab})$, define $P_b:=\\eta_{ba}P^a$, $J_{cd}:=\\eta_{ce}\\eta_{df}J^{ef}$, and $P^2:=\\eta_{ab}P^aP^b$. Let $\\varepsilon_{abcd}$ be the totally antisymmetric Minkowski volume tensor with $\\varepsilon_{0123}=+1$; raising all indices with $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ gives $\\varepsilon^{0123}=-1$. Define the algebraic vector $W^a:=\\tfrac12\\varepsilon^{abcd}P_bJ_{cd}$, and write $W^2:=\\eta_{ab}W^aW^b$ and $W\\cdot P:=\\eta_{ab}W^aP^b$. For fixed mass $m>0$ in kg, angular-momentum parameter $s>0$ in kg m$^2$/s, and $c>0$ in m/s, take the rest charge point with $P^a=(mc,0,0,0)$ and $J^{12}=s$, all other independent components of $J$ being zero. Its coadjoint orbit is an eight-dimensional smooth symplectic manifold with its KKS form. On this orbit, $P^2=-m^2c^2$, $P$ is future, $W\\cdot P=0$, and $W^2=m^2c^2s^2$. The exact joint invariant set with these values is this one orbit. At $s=0$ the rest stabilizer has dimension four and the spinless orbit has dimension six; no smoothness claim is made for a larger union of Casimir level sets.",
